X-SOURCE-URLS:{"Thedirt": "https://thedirt.online/event/above-bone/2026-11-07/"}
X-SOURCE-ID:thedirt
X-SOURCE:The Dirt
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261107T120000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261107T170000
DTSTAMP:20261017T202128Z
CREATED:20261017T202128Z
LAST-MODIFIED:20261017T202128Z
UID:10087076-1794052800-1794070800@thedirt.online
SUMMARY:Above Bone
DESCRIPTION:ADERO WILLARD\nABOVE BONE\nNovember 6–29\, 2026 \nReception and Artist Talk on Saturday\, November 7\, 5–8 PM\nArtist in the gallery on Sundays\, November 8 and 29\, 12–5 PM \nAxis Gallery presents Above Bone\, an exhibition of ceramic sculpture by Sacramento artist Adero Willard\, on view November 6 through 29\, 2026. The exhibition brings together new work and pieces never before shown in Sacramento or on the West Coast. Join the artist for the reception and artist talk on Saturday\, November 7\, 5–8 PM. \nABOUT THE EXHIBITION\nAbove Bone refers to what is beneath\, the layers\, and what emerges. Clay sculptural vessels of heads and body forms explore death\, regeneration\, new growth\, and what is hidden. Adero wrestles clay into expressive forms and creates surfaces dense in color and pattern: thick clay and delicately etched vines and flowers. The forms are camouflaged\, as if growing from or being returned to the ground. Layered colorful patterns\, resists\, and luster build up over multiple firings. Pattern is masked\, peeled back\, and built up again\, so each surface both hides and reveals. The work is rooted in the earth and in a mixed ancestry: ancestors from this land and from lands never touched\, removed from the land by history and time. It reflects a search for grounding\, authenticity\, and belonging. These pieces hold quiet conversations with personal history\, our current history\, and ancestors.\nThe exhibition moves through memory\, death\, decay\, and renewal. Patterns are hidden\, revealed\, and peeled; color surfaces from shadow. Drawing on textiles\, flowers\, plants\, and insects\, the work asks what grows from decay. New work appears alongside pieces making their Sacramento debut. \nABOUT THE ARTIST \nAdero Willard (they/them/she/her) is a ceramic artist\, educator\, and curator whose work explores surface\, identity\, and cultural memory through clay. Born in New York City\, Willard earned a BFA from the New York State College of Ceramics at Alfred University and an MFA from the Nova Scotia College of Art and Design in Halifax\, Canada. They have exhibited and lectured nationally and internationally and have been featured in numerous publications and books on contemporary ceramics. \nCurrently Assistant Professor of Ceramics at California State University\, Sacramento\, Willard is also a co-founder of Pots on Wheels!\, a nonprofit dedicated to expanding access to clay through community-based programs across the U.S. Willard’s artistic\, teaching\, \nand curatorial practices are grounded in a commitment to equity and to advocating for more inclusive institutional and cultural frameworks that reflect the richness and diversity of the field. \nMedia Contact\nAdero Willard · potterybyadero@gmail.com \nJoin us at Axis Gallery\, located at 625 S Street in Sacramento’s historic R Street Corridor\, within the Verge Center for the Arts building. The gallery has exhibited innovative contemporary art for over 35 years and continues to serve as a vital space for artists to explore\, connect\, and share work outside the commercial sphere. \nGallery hours: Friday–Sunday\, 12–5 PM
URL:https://thedirt.online/event/above-bone/2026-11-07/
LOCATION:Axis Gallery\, 625 S St\, Sacramento\, 95811\, United States
CATEGORIES:Art
ATTACH;FMTTYPE=image/jpeg:https://thedirt.online/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/Adero_-reemerge-Adero-Willard.jpg
ORGANIZER;CN="Axis Gallery":MAILTO:info@axisgallery.org
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE-URLS:{"The Dirt": "https://thedirt.online/event/capturing-memories-of-grief-capturando-memorias-del-duelo-2/"}
X-SOURCE-URLS:{"Thedirt": "https://thedirt.online/event/capturing-memories-of-grief-capturando-memorias-del-duelo/2026-11-07/"}
X-SOURCE-ID:thedirt
X-SOURCE:The Dirt
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261107T120000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261107T170000
DTSTAMP:20261017T203530Z
CREATED:20261017T203530Z
LAST-MODIFIED:20261017T203530Z
UID:10087245-1794052800-1794070800@thedirt.online
SUMMARY:Capturing Memories of Grief/Capturando Memorias del Duelo
DESCRIPTION:SUMMER VENTIS AND LINA RINCÓN\nCAPTURING MEMORIES OF GRIEF/CAPTURANDO MEMORIAS DEL DUELO\nNovember 6–29\, 2026 \nReception on Saturday\, November 7\, 5–8 PM \nThe project\, “Capturing Memories of Grief\,” is a collaboration between artist Summer Ventis\, sociologist and poet Lina Rincón\, and you. Together\, we explore the intersection of grief\, memory\, and image. \nWe began this project with a conversation about our shared experience of losing a parent. When we recalled these experiences\, we learned that we both had memories that centered around images\, photographs\, and the process of photography as a way to make sense of and process the meanings of our loss. \nIn Fall of 2025\, we began a series of grief-themed tours of works in the Crocker Art Museum’s collection. At the end of the tours\, participants gathered to share and document our memories of grief and loss and how they connect with different types of images. We invited participants to consider grief and loss broadly; while we first connected around the death of family members\, death is not the only kind of loss or the only thing we grieve. We encouraged participants to reflect on different experiences of grief and loss\, the images they associated with them\, and their feelings around these images. \nThis exhibition is a collection of the artifacts of these exchanges\, an opportunity to collect our experiences and reflect on them together. \n/ \nEl proyecto “Capturando memorias del duelo\,” es un proyecto colaborativo entre la artista Summer Ventis\, la socióloga y poeta Lina Rincón y usted. Juntes\, exploramos la intersección del duelo\, la memoria y la imágen.\nEste proyecto comenzó con una conversación sobre nuestra experiencia compartida de perder a uno de nuestros parientes. Cuando recordamos estas experiencias\, nos dimos cuenta que para darle sentido a los significados del duelo y procesarlos\, relatamos memorias que centraban imágenes\, fotografías\, y el proceso de la fotografía. \nEn el otoño del 2025\, empezamos una serie de recorridos de las obras de arte del Museo Crocker centrando el tema del duelo. Al final del recorrido\, los participantes compartieron y documentaron sus experiencias con memorias del duelo y sus conexiones con diferentes tipos de imágenes. En el recorrido\, invitamos a los participantes a considerar las experiencias del duelo y la pérdida de manera amplia; aunque es común pensar en la muerte de nuestros familiares primero\, la muerte no es la única manera de pensar en las experiencias de duelo y pérdida. De este modo\, motivamos a los participantes a reflexionar sobre sus diferentes experiencias con el duelo y la pérdida\, las imagenes que elles asociaron con esta experiencias\, y los sentimientos alrededor de estas imagenes. \nEsta exhibición es una colección de los artefactos de estos intercambios\, una oportunidad para recolectar nuestras experiencias\, y también para reflexionar sobre ellas. \nABOUT THE ARTIST \nSummer Ventis \nSummer Ventis’s work uses the printed surface to address internal and external landscapes and their intersections; the imprints we leave on each other and our surroundings and the imprints that our surroundings leave on us. She received a BA in Art from Grinnell College and an MFA in Printmaking from the University of Colorado Boulder. Her work has appeared in national and international exhibitions\, and is held by collections including those of the Denver Art Museum and Proyecto ’ace in Buenos Aires\, Argentina. She is a member of the Colorado-based collectives Hyperlink and ARTNAUTS and of Sacramento-based Axis Gallery and is Associate Professor of Printmaking at California State University Sacramento. \nLina Rincón\nAn immigrant herself\, Lina Rincón studies the experiences of migrant workers in the U.S. As part of her research\, Lina has revealed the detrimental effect different visa programs and associated policies have on families. This includes family temporary or permanent family separation. Lina also brings her artistry as a published poet who has captured experiences of loss and change in immigrant families in her poetry. She received a BA and a Masters Degree in Anthropology from Universidad de los Andes in Bogotá\, Colombia and a PhD in Sociology from the State University of New York at Albany. She is Associate Professor of Sociology at California State University Sacramento. \nSummer Ventis \nLa obra de Summer Ventis usa la superficie impresa para revelar paisajes internos y externos y sus intersecciones; las impresiones que dejamos en cada une de nosotres y nuestros alrededores y las impresiones que nuestros alrededores dejan en nosotres. Summer recibió su BA en artes de Grinnell College y un MFA en grabado de la Universidad de Colorado en Boulder. Su arte ha aparecido en exhibiciones nacionales e internacionales y ha sido adquirido por varias colecciones\, incluyendo las del Museo de Arte de Denver y el Proyecto ’ace en Buenos Aires\, Argentina. Summer hace parte de los colectivos Hyperlink y ARTNAUTS\, basados en Colorado\, y de la Galería Axis\, basada en Sacramento. Summer es profesora asociada de grabado en la Universidad Estatal de California en Sacramento. \nSummer: instagram: @summerventis\, summerventis.com; \nLina Rincón \nComo inmigrante que es\, Lina Rincón estudia las experiencias de trabajadores migrantes en los Estados Unidos. Como parte de su investigación\, Lina ha revelado los efectos perjudiciales que diferentes programas de visas de inmigración y sus políticas asociadas tienen en las familias inmigrantes. Estos efectos incluyen la separación temporal o permanente de miembros de estas familias. Lina es también una poeta que ha capturado experiencias de dolor y cambio en las experiencias de familias inmigrantes en su poesía. Lina recibió su BA y maestría en antropología de la Universidad de los Andes en Bogotá\, Colombia y su PhD en sociología de la Universidad Estatal de Nueva York en Albany. Lina es profesora asociada de sociología en la Universidad Estatal de California en Sacramento. \nLina: Instagram: @lioshenkamonarca \nJoin us at Axis Gallery\, located at 625 S Street in Sacramento’s historic R Street Corridor\, within the Verge Center for the Arts building. The gallery has exhibited innovative contemporary art for over 35 years and continues to serve as a vital space for artists to explore\, connect\, and share work outside the commercial sphere. \nGallery hours: Friday–Sunday\, 12–5 PM
URL:https://thedirt.online/event/capturing-memories-of-grief-capturando-memorias-del-duelo/2026-11-07/
LOCATION:Axis Gallery\, 625 S St\, Sacramento\, 95811\, United States
CATEGORIES:Art
ATTACH;FMTTYPE=image/jpeg:https://thedirt.online/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/VentisRincon_1-Summer-Ventis-1600x1281-1.jpeg
ORGANIZER;CN="Axis Gallery":MAILTO:info@axisgallery.org
END:VEVENT
